    "result_text": "The build succeeds and all tests pass (the acceptance suite is the only test module, and the full context \u2014 OpenAPI-generated `OwnerDto`, MapStruct mapper, OfficeFloor wiring \u2014 all compiled and started cleanly).\n\n## Summary\n\nI converted owner deletion to a soft-delete. The change touches the model, schema, response contract, and the two create-time checks:\n\n**Soft-delete state**\n- `Owner.java` \u2014 added a `deleted` `boolean` field (`@Column(name = \"deleted\", nullable = false)`) with `isDeleted()`/`setDeleted()`. As a primitive it defaults to `false`, so a newly created owner is not deleted.\n- Schema files (`db/hsqldb`, `db/h2`, `db/mysql`, `db/postgres`) \u2014 added `deleted BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE` so the seed `INSERT`s (which don't list the column) and Hibernate inserts both get `false`, never `NULL` into the primitive. Tests run on the **hsqldb** profile; the others are kept in sync.\n\n**Response contract**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added a `deleted` boolean property to the `Owner` schema (readonly) and to its `required` list, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct auto-maps it by name. `GET /api/owners/{id}` now returns `deleted`.\n\n**Behavior**\n- `DeleteOwner` \u2014 instead of `repository.delete(owner)`, it now sets `deleted = true` and `save`s, so the row is retained and `DELETE` returns 204 (2xx). GET afterward returns the owner with `deleted: true`.\n- `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique` \u2014 skips soft-deleted owners, so a duplicate is allowed when the only matching owner has been deleted.\n- `AssignPossibleDuplicate` \u2014 likewise skips soft-deleted owners when computing the possible-duplicate flag.\n\nI left the city-capacity (50) and daily-limit (100) checks unchanged: the spec scopes the deleted-owner exclusion to the duplicate/identity checks, and a single retained row can't trip those thresholds.\n\nTest result: `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `coreSoftDeleteFlagsAndRetains` and `coreDeletedOwnerIgnoredByDuplicateCheck` both green, `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
